# Cloudflare Pages response headers.
#
# Vite copies this file verbatim from `public/` to `dist/`; Pages consumes it as
# deployment configuration and does not serve it as an asset.
#
# ===========================================================================
# WHY THERE IS NO `/data/*` RULE — read this before "simplifying" the file
# ===========================================================================
#
# Cloudflare `_headers` does NOT do first-match-wins, and it does NOT let a
# specific rule beat a wildcard. Two sentences from the docs decide the whole
# design of this file:
#
#   "An incoming request which matches multiple rules' URL patterns will
#    inherit all rules' headers."
#   "If a header is applied twice in the `_headers` file, the values are
#    joined with a comma separator."
#   -- https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/headers/
#
# So the obvious spelling —
#
#     /data/data-manifest.json
#       Cache-Control: no-store
#     /data/*
#       Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
#
# does NOT give the manifest `no-store`. `/data/*` also matches
# `/data/data-manifest.json`, both rules fire, and the manifest goes out as:
#
#     Cache-Control: no-store, public, max-age=31536000, immutable
#
# — a self-contradictory header whose interpretation is up to each cache. That
# is not a bet worth taking on the one mutable file in the deployment: a cached
# manifest names a previous export's content-hashed files, so every drill-down
# 404s. Cloudflare also does NOT purge the CDN on deploy
# (https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/serving-pages/), so a
# stale manifest at the edge would persist for its full TTL.
#
# Therefore: **no rule in this file may match `/data/data-manifest.json` except
# the manifest's own rule.** The immutable policy is spelled out per
# subdirectory and per top-level export instead of as one `/data/*` wildcard.
# If the exporter ever adds a new top-level file under `data/`, add a rule for
# it here — the cost of forgetting is a revalidation round-trip, not a bug.
#
# Header negation (`! Cache-Control`) is documented and would allow a `/data/*`
# wildcard, but it is deliberately NOT used: there is an open report of
# negation being ignored (cloudflare/workers-sdk#2776), and the failure mode is
# exactly the one above.
#
# `_headers` values REPLACE the Pages defaults rather than appending to them —
# the default for a cacheable asset is `Cache-Control: public, max-age=0,
# must-revalidate`. That is the documented purpose of the file, but it is worth
# checking on the first deployment, because if it appended instead, the leading
# `max-age=0` would win and nothing here would be cached at all. See §4.1 of
# docs/deploy.md for the curl that settles it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The one mutable file in the whole deployment.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `data-manifest.json` maps logical names -> content-hashed paths for the
# CURRENT export. Every deploy replaces it. The client already fetches it with
# `cache: "no-store"`, which defeats the browser cache but NOT Cloudflare's
# edge cache. This rule is what defeats the edge cache.
/data/data-manifest.json
  Cache-Control: no-store

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything else under /data/ is content-addressed -> immutable by construction.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Filenames carry a hash of their own contents (`summary.d1fe50f6.json`,
# `shards/03018.f337e906.json.gz`), so a given URL can never change meaning.
# A new export writes new names and a new manifest; it never rewrites these.
#
# NOTE ON `.json.gz`: these hold raw gzip bytes and the client decompresses
# them itself, sniffing the gzip magic number (`1f 8b`) first. It is therefore
# correct whether the server hands over the compressed bytes or a body the
# browser already inflated because of a `Content-Encoding: gzip` response
# header. Content-Encoding and Content-Type are deliberately NOT set here:
# both servings work, Cloudflare manages Content-Encoding itself, and pinning
# the wrong one would break the serving that currently works. What Pages
# actually does with a `.gz` path is NOT documented — post-deploy verification
# checks it first. See §4.1 and §5 of docs/deploy.md.
/data/shards/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/data/anomalies/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/data/states/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Top-level hashed exports, one rule each. A splat cannot be replaced by a
# placeholder here: in a path, `:name` only stops at `/`, never at `.`, so
# `/data/summary.:hash.json` would not match `summary.d1fe50f6.json`.
/data/summary.*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/data/municipalities-index.*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/data/annulled-candidacies.*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Vite's build output. Also content-hashed (`assets/index-CwRJXZ0F.js`).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Map geometry: NOT content-hashed, so it cannot be immutable.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `geo/br-municipalities.topo.json` is election-independent and regenerated by
# hand (`scripts/build-geo.mjs`), keeping its name across rebuilds. A day of
# staleness on a boundary file nobody edits is a good trade for the 1.8 MB
# every map route fetches.
/geo/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The SPA shell. Must never be cached: it names the current hashed JS/CSS.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache
